    overview: "An action takes its value from the motive behind it. But motives often come in pairs. Ghazali names four patterns — with everyday examples — and this sorting becomes the key to the rest of the book.",
    thesis: "An action follows the motive behind it; when two motives combine, the second is either a partner that could act alone, a helper that makes it easier, or a co-worker neither could act without.",
